1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:08,779
What would you do if your future in-laws demanded fifty thousand dollars from your family, while your father was left standing at the table?

2
00:00:08,779 --> 00:00:12,848
Today's story is about a daughter who turned that night upside down.

3
00:00:13,748 --> 00:00:15,473
The Ridgewood Country Club.

4
00:00:15,473 --> 00:00:20,456
My father, a carpenter, wore the one suit he owns, the sleeves a little short.

5
00:00:20,456 --> 00:00:27,037
In the parking lot, my future mother-in-law asked him to move his old work truck next to the dumpsters.

6
00:00:27,037 --> 00:00:29,464
Inside, she never offered him a chair.

7
00:00:29,464 --> 00:00:31,636
Then she handed over a typed list.

8
00:00:31,636 --> 00:00:34,447
Bride's family contribution, fifty thousand.

9
00:00:34,447 --> 00:00:35,853
Flowers, six thousand.

10
00:00:35,853 --> 00:00:37,067
A fur coat for her.

11
00:00:37,067 --> 00:00:38,344
Nothing but numbers.

12
00:00:39,244 --> 00:00:43,976
Dad brought walnut serving spoons he had carved over three months of evenings.

13
00:00:43,976 --> 00:00:44,704
She laughed.

14
00:00:44,704 --> 00:00:46,888
"Love is measured in dollars, dear."

15
00:00:46,888 --> 00:00:50,527
The spoon rolled off the table, and Dad knelt to pick it up.

16
00:00:50,527 --> 00:00:52,347
My fiancé stared at his plate.

17
00:00:52,347 --> 00:00:55,137
My future father-in-law looked out the window.

18
00:00:55,137 --> 00:00:58,292
But under the table, I saw his fist, clenched white.

19
00:00:59,192 --> 00:01:00,928
I wanted to flip that table.

20
00:01:00,928 --> 00:01:03,905
But a scene would only get me called ungrateful.

21
00:01:03,905 --> 00:01:04,835
I needed proof.

22
00:01:04,835 --> 00:01:13,392
On the drive home, Dad held the spoons on his lap and finally said, "Watching them look down on you because of me, that's the worst part."

23
00:01:13,392 --> 00:01:16,989
That night, the light in his workshop stayed on until two.

24
00:01:17,889 --> 00:01:19,047
Here's my secret.

25
00:01:19,047 --> 00:01:22,044
I was living that night for the second time.

26
00:01:22,044 --> 00:01:24,428
In my first life, I kept the peace.

27
00:01:24,428 --> 00:01:31,920
I signed papers I never read, and my bakery became collateral for her secret two hundred thousand dollar loan.

28
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:36,416
When the collectors came, my father collapsed in front of my shop.

29
00:01:36,416 --> 00:01:38,936
And then I woke up, one year earlier.

30
00:01:39,836 --> 00:01:44,181
I heard Dad coughing in the kitchen, and the sound of sandpaper on wood.

31
00:01:44,181 --> 00:01:47,318
I ran in barefoot and hugged him like a little girl.

32
00:01:47,318 --> 00:01:50,456
"Getting cold feet about leaving your old man, huh?"

33
00:01:50,456 --> 00:01:53,534
This time, I promised myself, I would not lose him.

34
00:01:54,434 --> 00:01:55,717
I recorded everything.

35
00:01:55,717 --> 00:01:58,752
I photographed the so-called broker's business card.

36
00:01:58,752 --> 00:02:01,669
I signed up for the county's property fraud alert.

37
00:02:01,669 --> 00:02:06,571
Two weeks before the wedding, she pushed a mortgage on my building across the table.

38
00:02:06,571 --> 00:02:07,796
"Do not tell Richard.

39
00:02:07,796 --> 00:02:09,546
Sign, or forget this wedding."

40
00:02:09,546 --> 00:02:12,931
That night she texted, "Who does a carpenter think he is?"

41
00:02:13,831 --> 00:02:18,875
She had already mocked Dad's workshop and cut his name from the invitations.

42
00:02:18,875 --> 00:02:22,858
Then Dad said he would sell the workshop to raise the money.

43
00:02:22,858 --> 00:02:25,779
In my first life, he did, and he faded away.

44
00:02:25,779 --> 00:02:26,907
I took his hands.

45
00:02:26,907 --> 00:02:28,633
"Dad, give me three weeks.

46
00:02:28,633 --> 00:02:29,629
Just trust me."

47
00:02:30,529 --> 00:02:34,557
A few days later, my mother's ring was gone from Dad's drawer.

48
00:02:34,557 --> 00:02:40,598
I found him in the rain, outside the pawn shop on Main Street, holding the little velvet box.

49
00:02:40,598 --> 00:02:43,067
"This will get a few thousand, Gracie.

50
00:02:43,067 --> 00:02:44,821
Your mom would understand."

51
00:02:44,821 --> 00:02:51,708
I held him right there on the sidewalk, and for the first time in my life, I heard my father cry out loud.

52
00:02:51,708 --> 00:02:55,476
That was the day I decided to end this before the wedding.

53
00:02:56,376 --> 00:02:58,298
I called my future father-in-law.

54
00:02:58,298 --> 00:02:59,987
Seven a.m., a roadside diner.

55
00:02:59,987 --> 00:03:07,034
The man who owned half the buildings in the county stood up in a work jacket and shook my father's hand with both of his.

56
00:03:07,034 --> 00:03:09,248
"Tom, I didn't even offer you a chair.

57
00:03:09,248 --> 00:03:10,005
I'm ashamed."

58
00:03:10,005 --> 00:03:11,810
He listened to every recording.

59
00:03:11,810 --> 00:03:13,325
Then he called his lawyer.

60
00:03:14,225 --> 00:03:15,832
"I knew something was wrong,"

61
00:03:15,832 --> 00:03:16,275
he said.

62
00:03:16,275 --> 00:03:18,547
"She kept asking about the company cards.

63
00:03:18,547 --> 00:03:20,044
I pretended not to notice."

64
00:03:20,044 --> 00:03:24,810
In my first life, he was the one who told me at Dad's funeral, "I found out too late."

65
00:03:24,810 --> 00:03:26,473
This time, he wasn't too late.

66
00:03:27,373 --> 00:03:29,873
That night, she spread the list out again.

67
00:03:29,873 --> 00:03:31,302
Again, no chair for Dad.

68
00:03:31,302 --> 00:03:34,873
Richard carried a chair over himself and sat my father down.

69
00:03:34,873 --> 00:03:36,183
"Put that away, Diane.

70
00:03:36,183 --> 00:03:38,743
Before you embarrass yourself any further."

71
00:03:38,743 --> 00:03:42,255
The lawyer laid out the loan, the mortgage, the recordings.

72
00:03:42,255 --> 00:03:44,339
The broker had seven other victims.

73
00:03:44,339 --> 00:03:45,529
She hadn't invested.

74
00:03:45,529 --> 00:03:46,720
She'd been swindled.

75
00:03:47,620 --> 00:03:49,655
"Richard, please, just this once."

76
00:03:49,655 --> 00:03:50,613
His voice shook.

77
00:03:50,613 --> 00:03:52,229
"My father was a carpenter.

78
00:03:52,229 --> 00:03:54,384
This house started with one hammer."

79
00:03:54,384 --> 00:03:56,719
Then my fiancé finally stood beside me.

80
00:03:56,719 --> 00:03:58,934
"Mom, Grace never asked for anything.

81
00:03:58,934 --> 00:04:01,568
We were the ones trying to take her bakery."

82
00:04:02,468 --> 00:04:03,419
No contribution.

83
00:04:03,419 --> 00:04:05,858
Her lake house and jewelry paid her debt.

84
00:04:05,858 --> 00:04:08,119
She knelt and apologized to my father.

85
00:04:08,119 --> 00:04:15,436
On my wedding day, Dad walked me down the aisle in that same suit, and the favors were two hundred walnut spoons he carved.

86
00:04:15,436 --> 00:04:17,340
Love is not measured in dollars.

87
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:20,314
Tell us in the comments: what would you have done?
